1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,177
This is a technical demo of the ODELIA viewer, a breast M R I reading environment with an image classification model attached.

2
00:00:08,177 --> 00:00:10,036
We start by signing in.

3
00:00:10,036 --> 00:00:13,258
After signing in you get the study list.

4
00:00:13,258 --> 00:00:17,688
One row per study, with patient, date, modality and image count.

5
00:00:17,688 --> 00:00:20,507
We open the breast M R I.

6
00:00:20,507 --> 00:00:22,946
Expanding the row shows the reading modes.

7
00:00:22,946 --> 00:00:27,823
A I Analysis Mode opens the study with the analysis panel next to it.

8
00:00:27,823 --> 00:00:32,526
In the viewport, the series for this study are listed on the left.

9
00:00:32,526 --> 00:00:35,059
This one is a dynamic contrast series.

10
00:00:35,059 --> 00:00:37,539
Scrolling moves through the slice stack.

11
00:00:37,539 --> 00:00:40,979
Selecting the window level tool and dragging changes the contrast.

12
00:00:40,979 --> 00:00:42,979
The image zooms in and out.

13
00:00:42,979 --> 00:00:48,819
The corners of the viewport show the current window width and level, and the slice position in the stack.

14
00:00:48,819 --> 00:00:55,680
Results from the model are listed in the study panel, one entry per run, with the date and time.

15
00:00:55,680 --> 00:01:00,375
A study can carry several runs, from different models or on different days.

16
00:01:00,375 --> 00:01:02,256
Selecting a run loads it.

17
00:01:02,256 --> 00:01:08,651
It expands to show the attention heatmap it produced, and the structured report carrying both breast results.

18
00:01:08,651 --> 00:01:17,364
The overlay at the top of the viewport shows that run's output: its label, and for each breast the predicted class and its confidence.

19
00:01:17,364 --> 00:01:22,447
It stays on screen while you scroll, so the prediction travels with the images.

20
00:01:22,447 --> 00:01:26,289
An attention heatmap can be switched on alongside the images.

21
00:01:26,289 --> 00:01:29,747
Scrolling through it shows which regions the model weighted.

22
00:01:29,747 --> 00:01:34,358
The warmer colours are the areas that contributed most to the prediction.

23
00:01:34,358 --> 00:01:42,598
The feedback panel shows the same result and lets the reader respond: agree, unsure, or disagree, recorded for each breast separately.

24
00:01:42,598 --> 00:01:48,758
Previous and next step through the runs on this study, so an earlier result can be reviewed too.

25
00:01:48,758 --> 00:01:51,558
Submitting saves the response against that run.

26
00:01:51,558 --> 00:01:54,565
New runs are started from the same panel.

27
00:01:54,565 --> 00:01:57,196
Pick the model, here M S T.

28
00:01:57,196 --> 00:02:01,707
Pick the input mode, then map the series onto the model's input.

29
00:02:01,707 --> 00:02:07,345
Confirm and run, and the new result appears in the list when it is done.

30
00:02:07,345 --> 00:02:09,352
The third panel is chat.

31
00:02:09,352 --> 00:02:17,381
It runs against MedGemma, a medical vision language model, so it reads the images themselves rather than only the report.

32
00:02:17,381 --> 00:02:21,943
Everything the model is given is listed under prompt context.

33
00:02:21,943 --> 00:02:27,417
This study is one series, thirty one slices across five temporal positions.

34
00:02:27,417 --> 00:02:29,200
The context follows the viewer.

35
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:36,333
Scrolling the stack moves the range of slices that will be sent, and the window and level travel with it.

36
00:02:36,333 --> 00:02:40,413
You choose how much of it to send, and the slice count adjusts up or down.

37
00:02:40,413 --> 00:02:45,693
The temporal position picks which of the five contrast phases goes to the model.

38
00:02:45,693 --> 00:02:49,293
A region can be drawn to restrict the model to part of the image.

39
00:02:49,293 --> 00:02:51,293
The question goes in at the bottom.

40
00:02:51,293 --> 00:03:00,493
The answer comes back grounded in the slices that were actually sent, and the panel records which model answered and how many images went with it.

41
00:03:00,493 --> 00:03:03,259
This is a demo build, for research use.

42
00:03:03,259 --> 00:03:07,409
It is not a medical device and is not for diagnostic use.
